{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/12863-how-important-is-population-in-determining-what-courts-rule-a-freehold","id":12863,"title":"How important is population in determining what courts rule a Freehold?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-17T12:14:09-08:00","id":12863,"title":"How important is population in determining what courts rule a Freehold?","content":"So how important is population numbers of specific courts in determining what courts rule a freehold? For instance let me give an example. Say a normal American Freehold in say California has a couple score changelings and has been using the Seasonal court since before the Great War. Then lets say a nearby Freehold in Chinatown is wiped out by a mysterious Keeper assault, and another Freehold by the University town nearby that uses the Sun and Mooncourt system is wiped out by a Hunter revolution. Both these new courts swarm into the Seasonal Freehold who accepts them due to an open policy. Lets say both Refugee groups each outnumber the original Freehold's population. How do you determine the new court system? The seasonal court has tradition, but sheer population numbers now change the local culture.<br /> <br /> <br /> Similar situation what about the Dusk and Dawn Courts? In what situation do they get representational rule in a Freehold?<br /> <br /> Ultimately its up to the storyteller but what reasoning would you'all use?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-17T15:20:12-08:00","id":12995,"title":"","content":"It may seem like a cop-out, but the only true answer to questions like these is: It depends.<br /> <br /> If the refugees are adamant about keeping their old courts, there's going to be problem with them forcing their system on others.<br /> If the Seasonal court insists on keeping their own system, they'll likely give the others an option of joining a court before entering, or simply not letting them in (despite the open policy).<br /> If they don't really care, they could develop an overarching Seasonal Court + other Sub Courts.<br /> Or one side could just decide to kill everyone else.<br /> Or the sudden change could send them all into the Hedge, spiraling out of Clarity until they're picked up by Keepers and privateers. Changelings don't like change, it doesn't go well with them.<br /> Personally I'd have to figure out who the people in the courts were, what their attitudes were like, the various situations that just killed two freeholds, why the two freeholds that have more people than the Seasonal one would bother trying to join the Seasonal one, when they could just make their own, etc. It depends. On the story, on the characters, on the setting (some play world of darkness fairly lighthearted, some play it as brutal as possible), on vagaries of fate and whim, on all sorts of things.<br /> <br /> In the end, it's as important as it needs to be, for the story. There's no overarching answer just like in real life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}